Welcome back to Medical Engineering.

2
00:00:06,360 --> 00:00:11,000
And today we want to continue our little excursion through the theory.

3
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:15,400
But you'll see that we'll get much more applied in the next couple of videos.

4
00:00:15,400 --> 00:00:23,400
Because in the next two videos we want to talk about actual 2D signal processing.

5
00:00:23,400 --> 00:00:29,040
And the first thing that we want to look at is an introduction into 2D image processing.

6
00:00:29,040 --> 00:00:36,400
And we'll start with simple operations and expand them to linear systems theory and filters.

7
00:00:36,400 --> 00:00:41,480
And then in a second step we will look into non-linear systems.

8
00:00:41,480 --> 00:00:45,200
So I hope you enjoy the 2D image processing with me.

9
00:00:59,040 --> 00:01:16,760
So here we are with the medical engineering systems.

10
00:01:16,760 --> 00:01:20,840
And today the topic will be image processing.

11
00:01:20,840 --> 00:01:24,880
So let's have a look at the basics of image processing.

12
00:01:24,880 --> 00:01:30,440
Now an image is essentially a 2D function.

13
00:01:30,440 --> 00:01:36,520
And you can think of the image as a function that essentially is on a grid space where

14
00:01:36,520 --> 00:01:42,760
you could say essentially every column and every row is a function or a signal as we've

15
00:01:42,760 --> 00:01:44,320
discussed earlier.

16
00:01:44,320 --> 00:01:50,600
And now we have to expand the space and to be able to work with these 2D signals.

17
00:01:50,600 --> 00:01:53,520
Here on the right hand side you see an visualization.

18
00:01:53,520 --> 00:02:00,040
And you can see here that we have the red function which is essentially one column.

19
00:02:00,040 --> 00:02:04,640
And this is a sequence of values that you can see here plotted.

20
00:02:04,640 --> 00:02:09,080
And we also have the blue function which is essentially one row and it's again a sequence

21
00:02:09,080 --> 00:02:11,280
of values.

22
00:02:11,280 --> 00:02:16,960
Together these gray values here in this example form a 2D image.

23
00:02:16,960 --> 00:02:23,920
Now you see the lady here that is actually Lena and this is one of the first images that

24
00:02:23,920 --> 00:02:26,160
was used for image processing.

25
00:02:26,160 --> 00:02:33,960
It actually originates from a magazine that was published in November 1972.

26
00:02:33,960 --> 00:02:40,320
So at that time there were not so many scanners and digital images available.

27
00:02:40,320 --> 00:02:44,560
It happened that one of those images were scanned.

28
00:02:44,560 --> 00:02:50,120
So they took a subsection of one of the largest images that they could find in the magazine.

29
00:02:50,120 --> 00:02:56,840
They scanned it and actually this image became kind of iconic and has been used in image

30
00:02:56,840 --> 00:02:59,680
processing over and over again.

31
00:02:59,680 --> 00:03:06,160
So Lena became extremely popular and she has also been invited although she didn't know

32
00:03:06,160 --> 00:03:09,960
about her fans in the image processing community.

33
00:03:09,960 --> 00:03:17,040
So she has been invited actually to a couple of conferences and she is still being welcomed

34
00:03:17,040 --> 00:03:20,960
warmheartedly on various conferences.

35
00:03:20,960 --> 00:03:26,920
And today people still use this image in order to test their algorithms.

36
00:03:26,920 --> 00:03:33,240
Well actually there's many other image sets that people experiment with now but sometimes

37
00:03:33,240 --> 00:03:37,520
for nostalgic reasons this image is still being used.

38
00:03:37,520 --> 00:03:43,720
Note that only this subsection that we are showing here is being used and the full image

39
00:03:43,720 --> 00:03:50,000
is not being commonly used in the image processing community.

40
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:55,600
So just that you don't get the wrong impression it was just one of the images that was lying

41
00:03:55,600 --> 00:04:02,040
around and that they had access to and they decided to use the subsection of the image.

42
00:04:02,040 --> 00:04:04,600
Well what's the digital image?

43
00:04:04,600 --> 00:04:11,280
So let's go back and think of our function space and what you can see here is a highly

44
00:04:11,280 --> 00:04:12,720
magnified image.

45
00:04:12,720 --> 00:04:20,400
So this is just showing a couple of black and gray dots and what you see here is that

46
00:04:20,400 --> 00:04:26,520
essentially every color every shade of gray is associated to a value.

47
00:04:26,520 --> 00:04:32,160
So the gray that you can see here it's a light gray so it's associated to the value 200 and

48
00:04:32,160 --> 00:04:38,560
the dark color the black is associated in this example to the color 35.

49
00:04:38,560 --> 00:04:46,560
So essentially every shade of gray is associated to a number and this number originated from

50
00:04:46,560 --> 00:04:51,200
a sampling process as we discussed in the last video.

51
00:04:51,200 --> 00:04:56,960
Obviously these are the images that are used to introduce image processing what we will

52
00:04:56,960 --> 00:05:01,960
work with much more frequently of course medical images.

53
00:05:01,960 --> 00:05:09,160
And one thing that is very peculiar about medical images is that they often have more

54
00:05:09,160 --> 00:05:15,160
gray values more shades of gray that you can actually show at the same time on your monitor

55
00:05:15,160 --> 00:05:18,960
and actually more than you can perceive.

56
00:05:18,960 --> 00:05:28,760
So the human visual system can approximately differentiate 256 different shades of gray.

57
00:05:28,760 --> 00:05:33,440
You cannot differentiate more or the average human being cannot differentiate more and

58
00:05:33,440 --> 00:05:41,120
if you display them you will see that they appear as a continuous color gradient or gray

59
00:05:41,120 --> 00:05:44,660
or gray value gradient on your screen.

60
00:05:44,660 --> 00:05:50,760
So in order to be able to visualize all of the information that is in the medical image

61
00:05:50,760 --> 00:05:53,040
we have to use tricks.

62
00:05:53,040 --> 00:05:58,520
One trick that you could come up with is using different gamma values and you see them here

63
00:05:58,640 --> 00:06:04,560
we can do a gamma correction which is a non-linear scaling of the gray values and if we would

64
00:06:04,560 --> 00:06:12,720
do so we would be able to focus more on the skull or on the brain and change the color

65
00:06:12,720 --> 00:06:14,400
values.

66
00:06:14,400 --> 00:06:18,880
This however is not so frequently used in medical imaging.

67
00:06:18,880 --> 00:06:23,800
What is much more common is so called window leveling.

68
00:06:23,800 --> 00:06:30,160
So here we have an example of a CT image and we know that houndsfield units denoted

69
00:06:30,160 --> 00:06:38,240
as HU here are associated to different materials and on the left hand side and the right hand

70
00:06:38,240 --> 00:06:44,480
side you see exactly the same image but displayed in a different window level.

71
00:06:44,480 --> 00:06:52,440
So minus a thousand houndsfield units is air and plus a thousand houndsfield units is bone

72
00:06:52,440 --> 00:06:58,560
or contrast agent and you see here that we can visualize all of the materials on the

73
00:06:58,560 --> 00:07:05,680
left hand side ranging from air up to contrast agent that you can see in the heart and the

74
00:07:05,680 --> 00:07:13,160
bones and then on the right hand side image waterish materials such as water and soft

75
00:07:13,160 --> 00:07:20,560
tissues are around zero houndsfield units and if we pick a fresh out that is above zero

76
00:07:20,560 --> 00:07:26,000
houndsfield units only the contrast agent and the bone will be visible.

77
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:31,800
So what you see in this image is the contrast agent in the heart and the bones and you see

78
00:07:31,800 --> 00:07:37,800
that this is an interventional scan so there is still some residual artifact that you see

79
00:07:37,800 --> 00:07:45,240
at the bottom of the scan and this was caused by truncation at the end of the volume.

80
00:07:45,240 --> 00:07:52,560
So also sometimes these scans have associated artifacts and you have to know about the problems

81
00:07:52,560 --> 00:07:58,580
that can arise in the scanning process in order not to misread the information here.

82
00:07:58,580 --> 00:08:06,400
So for sure there is no ring like bone structure at the bottom of the scan here but this is

83
00:08:06,400 --> 00:08:12,160
artificially created by the reconstruction method and if you're reading these images

84
00:08:12,160 --> 00:08:15,720
if you're interpreting these images you have to know about this.

85
00:08:15,720 --> 00:08:21,960
Obviously there is a wide range of variations that you can pick for windowing and thresholding

86
00:08:21,960 --> 00:08:28,280
and this is why I have a short video where we are playing with the window and threshold

87
00:08:28,280 --> 00:08:30,120
in different settings.

88
00:08:42,160 --> 00:09:12,120
So what you see is that the image is actually composed of different gray values.

89
00:09:12,120 --> 00:09:20,320
Now what I'm showing here is called a histogram and a histogram is essentially a kind of probability

90
00:09:20,320 --> 00:09:27,680
distribution because you count the frequency the occurrence of every gray value.

91
00:09:27,680 --> 00:09:37,680
So on the left hand side example we have gray values from 0 to 255 so 256 different shades

92
00:09:37,680 --> 00:09:48,160
of gray and you can see on the y-axis how often that how often that respective color

93
00:09:48,160 --> 00:09:52,360
or gray value appeared in the image.

94
00:09:52,360 --> 00:09:59,360
So the histogram doesn't contain the spatial information anymore but it tells you which

95
00:09:59,360 --> 00:10:03,360
gray value occurs how frequently.

96
00:10:03,360 --> 00:10:08,960
Now if we change the window and level what we're essentially doing is we're adjusting

97
00:10:08,960 --> 00:10:16,960
the histogram and here on the right hand side you can see a rescaling of the same histogram

98
00:10:16,960 --> 00:10:19,820
to only 16 bins.

99
00:10:19,820 --> 00:10:27,440
So here we have pooled neighboring gray values in order to reduce the number of gray values

100
00:10:27,440 --> 00:10:30,280
in that particular image.

101
00:10:30,280 --> 00:10:35,600
Basically this would have a large impact on the displayed image and you could immediately

102
00:10:35,600 --> 00:10:39,560
see that I adjusted the number of gray values.

103
00:10:39,560 --> 00:10:43,920
So this is something that we won't do in the following.

104
00:10:43,920 --> 00:10:46,480
Now let's go back to our example image.

105
00:10:46,480 --> 00:10:52,320
So here you see our famous Lennar and if you look at the associated histogram here on the

106
00:10:52,320 --> 00:11:00,000
right hand side you see that actually not all of the 256 gray values actually appear

107
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:01,600
in the image.

108
00:11:01,600 --> 00:11:08,160
So you see that there is a short area with very low gray values and very high gray values

109
00:11:08,160 --> 00:11:15,480
where the frequency is zero so they never appear in the image and you can see also that

110
00:11:15,480 --> 00:11:22,880
our Lennar well the image impression is kind of okay but she could use a little bit more

111
00:11:22,880 --> 00:11:25,280
contrast right.

112
00:11:25,280 --> 00:11:29,600
So this is where histogram equalization comes in.

113
00:11:29,600 --> 00:11:37,040
In histogram equalization the idea is that we want to optimally use all of the associated

114
00:11:37,040 --> 00:11:39,120
gray values.

115
00:11:39,120 --> 00:11:44,320
What we're doing here is we're showing the cumulative density function which means that

116
00:11:44,320 --> 00:11:49,840
we're essentially computing the integral over the histogram.

117
00:11:49,840 --> 00:11:57,640
So at every point in the cumulative density function I am showing the addition of all

118
00:11:57,640 --> 00:12:02,860
the values from the zero of bin to the current bin.

119
00:12:02,860 --> 00:12:09,440
So this can be computed essentially with a for loop where you then add up all of the

120
00:12:09,440 --> 00:12:14,160
previous values and write them into the respective bin.

121
00:12:14,160 --> 00:12:20,800
Now if I normalize this with the total number of pixels in the image you will see that we

122
00:12:20,800 --> 00:12:24,840
get values between zero and one.

123
00:12:24,840 --> 00:12:31,560
If you look at the left hand side image you see the cumulative density function of our

124
00:12:31,560 --> 00:12:38,360
original image and you can also see the plateaus at the very low values and at the very high

125
00:12:38,360 --> 00:12:44,280
values where we don't have any change in gray values anymore because these gray values

126
00:12:44,280 --> 00:12:47,560
simply do not occur in the image.

127
00:12:47,560 --> 00:12:53,520
Now the idea of histogram equalization is that you transform this cumulative density

128
00:12:53,520 --> 00:12:57,440
function on the left into the one on the right.

129
00:12:57,440 --> 00:13:04,960
So we want to linearize the entire cumulative density function which essentially means that

130
00:13:04,960 --> 00:13:12,640
we try to achieve a more or less uniform distribution of the gray values.

131
00:13:12,640 --> 00:13:20,920
There is algorithms to do so and in particular they then introduce small gaps in the histogram

132
00:13:20,920 --> 00:13:25,640
such that all of the gray values are being used.

133
00:13:25,640 --> 00:13:30,960
So you could say this is very similar to what we are achieving when we apply the window

134
00:13:30,960 --> 00:13:33,040
leveling.

135
00:13:33,040 --> 00:13:40,680
Now here you can see a histogram that has been equalized and now the contrast of the

136
00:13:40,680 --> 00:13:46,520
image has been improved and you can see that our lowest gray value is now really zero and

137
00:13:46,520 --> 00:13:51,160
the highest gray value is really 255.

138
00:13:51,160 --> 00:13:56,080
Let's compare the two images and here you can clearly see that the right hand side image

139
00:13:56,080 --> 00:14:02,000
has a much better contrast than the left hand side image.

140
00:14:02,000 --> 00:14:08,520
Good so these are the very basics of image processing and now we want to go ahead and

141
00:14:08,520 --> 00:14:13,320
look what we can achieve with linear systems theory.

142
00:14:13,320 --> 00:14:19,280
And one thing that can be very nicely achieved is computing derivatives.

143
00:14:19,280 --> 00:14:25,280
Now at the derivative of a function if you look at the definition this is essentially

144
00:14:25,280 --> 00:14:35,040
the limit of the function plus a small value at the position x and then you subtract the

145
00:14:35,040 --> 00:14:41,280
original value at position x from that and divide by the small value h.

146
00:14:41,280 --> 00:14:48,760
If you now have the limit of h towards zero you get the continuous derivative.

147
00:14:48,760 --> 00:14:52,760
Now the problem that we have is that we are in a discrete space.

148
00:14:52,760 --> 00:14:59,040
In a discrete space we cannot assign values of h that are lower than one because they

149
00:14:59,040 --> 00:15:04,560
would be zero and then our whole computation would simply collapse to zero.

150
00:15:04,560 --> 00:15:11,160
So what we do is we then choose different variants of doing a single a step size of

151
00:15:11,160 --> 00:15:15,800
one and then you come up with the solution shown here at the bottom.

152
00:15:15,800 --> 00:15:25,720
So you can for example take the value of one for h then you add one to the function f of

153
00:15:25,720 --> 00:15:30,240
x and subtract f of x from that.

154
00:15:30,240 --> 00:15:33,040
This would be the forward difference.

155
00:15:33,040 --> 00:15:38,560
There is the backward difference where you set h to minus one so you subtract one then

156
00:15:38,560 --> 00:15:45,640
the two flip and you have f of x minus f of x minus one.

157
00:15:45,640 --> 00:15:50,920
So the problem with those two approaches is that you are essentially computing the derivative

158
00:15:50,920 --> 00:15:55,400
at the position x plus 0.5.

159
00:15:55,400 --> 00:16:00,160
So you are kind of shifting the coordinate system and you wouldn't be in the original

160
00:16:00,160 --> 00:16:06,400
coordinate system which is the reason why very frequently there is also central differences

161
00:16:06,400 --> 00:16:12,840
and the central difference then simply takes the pixel on the right hand side and subtracts

162
00:16:12,840 --> 00:16:17,840
pixel on the left hand side of the current position of the image.

163
00:16:17,840 --> 00:16:23,600
This is interesting because it allows us to compute image derivatives.

164
00:16:23,600 --> 00:16:27,800
Now why would we be interested in image derivatives?

165
00:16:27,800 --> 00:16:34,440
Well let's look at an example and here you can see the derivatives in x and y direction

166
00:16:34,440 --> 00:16:39,480
and what you see very nicely here is that the edges pop up.

167
00:16:39,480 --> 00:16:46,040
So edges in our image are locations of very high gradients.

168
00:16:46,040 --> 00:16:52,080
So here you have either in x or y directions very high derivatives.

169
00:16:52,080 --> 00:17:00,560
So one idea for example to detect edges is to compute the derivative and x and y direction

170
00:17:00,560 --> 00:17:03,900
and then compute the magnitude of the two.

171
00:17:03,900 --> 00:17:10,700
So because you have a two dimensional function you will be introducing a gradient that is

172
00:17:10,700 --> 00:17:15,220
also two dimensional because you compute the derivatives in x and y direction and then

173
00:17:15,220 --> 00:17:22,860
you take the norm for example the L2 norm of this gradient and this way you can determine

174
00:17:22,860 --> 00:17:26,220
the magnitude and detect edges.

175
00:17:26,220 --> 00:17:32,700
Also interesting is that you can also compute the direction and then you could compute the

176
00:17:32,700 --> 00:17:41,460
angle of the edges which then allows you also to detect edges of a certain orientation.

177
00:17:41,460 --> 00:17:49,540
So this is a very very common application for linear systems and a good way of how we

178
00:17:49,540 --> 00:17:55,660
can actually compute edges in an image.

179
00:17:55,660 --> 00:17:59,620
This brings us back to image filtering and linear systems theory.

180
00:17:59,620 --> 00:18:07,540
So you remember that a filter can be applied to an image and this filter has very similar

181
00:18:07,540 --> 00:18:12,220
properties as we already have discussed in the 1D case.

182
00:18:12,220 --> 00:18:17,440
So the filters are linear and they are shift invariant.

183
00:18:17,440 --> 00:18:25,620
So this is a linear shift invariant system which means that it can be entirely described

184
00:18:25,620 --> 00:18:28,140
by convolution.

185
00:18:28,140 --> 00:18:35,140
And therefore we will need a 2D convolution and now you can see here that we have some

186
00:18:35,140 --> 00:18:43,900
function k which is a filter kernel and the filter kernel now takes the role of the impulse

187
00:18:43,900 --> 00:18:50,540
response that we talked about earlier in our systems theory.

188
00:18:50,540 --> 00:18:57,340
So now we have suddenly 2D impulse responses and these can be written up in a discrete

189
00:18:57,340 --> 00:19:01,820
fashion and then we end up with filter kernels.

190
00:19:01,820 --> 00:19:08,380
For some functions they can be described in a very compact way such as for the derivative

191
00:19:08,380 --> 00:19:13,580
we've seen that every point of the image we essentially have to take the left neighbor

192
00:19:13,580 --> 00:19:21,180
and the right neighbor subtract the two from each other and then we get exactly the derivative

193
00:19:21,180 --> 00:19:23,580
in that particular direction.

194
00:19:23,580 --> 00:19:31,220
So that's interesting because we've just seen that computing a derivative can be performed

195
00:19:31,220 --> 00:19:38,780
by convolution and for a derivative we can determine a kernel that is performing the

196
00:19:38,780 --> 00:19:40,740
derivative.

197
00:19:40,740 --> 00:19:48,780
So this means that computing derivatives is something that can be explained by a linear

198
00:19:48,780 --> 00:19:51,220
shift invariant system.

199
00:19:51,220 --> 00:19:55,060
So that's actually pretty useful and you will see that throughout your studies this

200
00:19:55,060 --> 00:20:00,460
will have many many applications and it's also very useful for example for solving partial

201
00:20:00,460 --> 00:20:02,940
differential equations.

202
00:20:02,940 --> 00:20:09,260
So you will see this will come up over and over again in your studies.

203
00:20:09,260 --> 00:20:14,300
Here we will now only stick to the discrete version and the nice thing with the discrete

204
00:20:14,300 --> 00:20:20,220
version is that we can write these up as these filter kernels and you will see that this

205
00:20:20,220 --> 00:20:28,980
then takes the form of very small masks for several filters such as the derivative filter.

206
00:20:28,980 --> 00:20:34,900
A side remark of course linear systems theory applies and also the fundamental properties

207
00:20:34,900 --> 00:20:44,260
apply which means that if you have a filter kernel and you convert it into Fourier domain

208
00:20:44,260 --> 00:20:48,940
and you have the image and you convert it to 2D Fourier domain then you just have to

209
00:20:48,940 --> 00:20:56,260
multiply the two point wise and convert back and you do exactly a convolution.

210
00:20:56,260 --> 00:21:02,300
Now for the simple filters that we have here in this set of slides this would be rather

211
00:21:02,300 --> 00:21:08,140
excessive because typically the kernels are very small they are like one by three or three

212
00:21:08,140 --> 00:21:10,060
by three or five by five.

213
00:21:10,060 --> 00:21:15,000
These are really compact kernels and there it doesn't make sense to compute the complete

214
00:21:15,000 --> 00:21:16,420
Fourier transform.

215
00:21:16,420 --> 00:21:22,500
But sometimes you have kernels with very large extent in spatial domain and there it makes

216
00:21:22,500 --> 00:21:27,140
sense to make use of the Fourier properties here.

217
00:21:27,140 --> 00:21:32,740
Also you will see that the 2D Fourier space is something that comes up in this class again

218
00:21:32,740 --> 00:21:34,860
and again.

219
00:21:34,860 --> 00:21:38,500
Now what's actually a 2D Fourier space?

220
00:21:38,500 --> 00:21:44,700
You remember that when we are computing the 1D Fourier transform we essentially take a

221
00:21:44,700 --> 00:21:53,580
signal of a fixed frequency and then we multiply it point wise with the signal that we want

222
00:21:53,580 --> 00:22:00,980
to process sum everything up and we get the coefficient for that particular frequency.

223
00:22:00,980 --> 00:22:09,820
Now in the 2D case this is slightly different because now our 2D waves have to be described

224
00:22:09,820 --> 00:22:16,340
also in 2D which means that it's not just the frequency of the wave but they also have

225
00:22:16,340 --> 00:22:17,580
an orientation.

226
00:22:17,580 --> 00:22:23,780
So they are essentially sine and cosine wave fronts that have a particular orientation

227
00:22:23,780 --> 00:22:31,140
and you can see that they are printed here the base functions on this set of slides and

228
